Group A Short Questions

1. Define Information Architecture.
ans: Information Architecture is the process of organizing , structuring and labeling information in a clear and logical way so that the users can easily find , understand and use the information within a website , application or information system.

2.  What is a ”Wireframe”?
ans: A wireframe is a basic visual representation or a blueprint of a webpage that shows the placement of elements like menus , buttons , and content without design details such as colors and images . 

3. Explain the concept of ”Cognitive Friction” in web design.
ans: cognitive Friction refers to the mental effort that the users experience when a website os confusing , unckear or difficult to navigate making tasks harder top complete . 

4. Why should URL slugs use hyphens instead of underscores?
ans: URL slugs should use hyphens(-) instead of underscores(_) because hypens improve readability and SEO because search engines treat hyphens as word separators whereas underscores are treated as join words together.

Group B: Long Questions 

5. Compare and contrast Hierarchical (Tree) structure and Linear (Sequential) structure. Give an example of when to use each.
ans: A hierarchical (tree) structure organizes website content in a parent-child relationship , where main pages branch into sub pages. it allows users to navigate through categories and subcategories . 

A linear(Sequential) structure organizes contents in a fixed , step by step order where users move form one page to the next . it is simple and easy to follow but limited in navigation options.

Comparison:
i. Hierarchical structure offers flexible navigation , while linear structure follows fixed path.
ii. Hierarchical structure is suitable for large websites , while linear structure is ideal for small or guided processes .
iii. Hierarchical structure is complex in design, while linear structure is easy to understand.
iv. In Hierarchical structure users can quickly locate specific contents , wjile in linear structure users can only follow fixed path.

Examples:
Hierarchical structure: University website(Admissions→ Programs→Departmets).
Linear structure: online tutorial or checkout process.

6. Explain the ”Three-Click Rule” and its significance in User Experience (UX) design.
ans: The "Three-Click Rule" states that users chould be able to find any information on a website within three clicks. if the users takes more than three clicks, they may become frustrated and leave the site.

Significance in UX Design:
i.Improves Nvigation Efficiency 
  •Reduces the time and effort users spend searching for specific information.
ii.Enhances User Satisfaction
  •Users feel the website is easy to use and intuitive. 
  •Leads to higher trust and positive perception towards the website.
iii.Reduce Bounce Rate 
  •If users cannot find what they need quickly, they leave the site.
  •following the three-click rule helps retain visitors.
iv.Supports logical information Architecture
  •Encourages designers to organize content in a clear , hierarchical structure.

  Example:
  Good UX: Homepage→Serves→Web Design→Portfolio
  Bad UX: Hoepage→About us→Services→Web Design→Portfolio

  7.“Plan before you do.” Explain how tools like Card Sorting and Flowcharts help in planning a website’s structure.
  ans: "Plan Before you do" emphasizes the importance of planning a website before development begins. Card Sorting and Fowcharts are effective tools that can be used in the planning stage.

  Card Sorting
  Definition:Card Sorting involves writing content items on cards and grouping them based on similarity, it helps designers understand how users expect information to be organized and improves navigation structure.
  Purpose:Helps determine how users expect information to be organized.
  How it helps:
  •Revals logical categories for menus and pages.
  •Ensures the website's structure matches user expectations.

  Flowchart
  Definition:Flowchart visually represent the structure and navigation paths of a website. they show how pages are connected and how users move form one page to another.
  Purpose:Helps visualize navigation paths and relationships between pages.
  How it helps:
  •Shows how users move form one page to another.
  •Identifies redundancies , dead-ends or missing links.